        <div class="kpi-value">150</div>
        <div class="kpi-label">MCQs (45.9%)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="kpi-value">147</div>
        <div class="kpi-label">Short Questions (44.9%)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="kpi-value">30</div>
        <div class="kpi-label">Long Questions (9.2%)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="kpi-value">2015–2025</div>
        <div class="kpi-label">Year Range (2020 missing)</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Data Scope & Limitations</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The year <strong>2020 is absent</strong> from the dataset, likely due to examination cancellations or disruptions related to the COVID-19 pandemic. No 2020 paper data was available at the source.</li>
      <li>The dataset does not distinguish between annual and supplementary examination papers; both may be included for some years.</li>
      <li>Questions use a <code>$</code> delimiter to separate MCQ answer options and Long question sub-parts, which has been accounted for in all text analyses.</li>
      <li>This is a community-compiled, non-official dataset; minor transcription errors may exist.</li>
      <li>With 327 total questions, the dataset is compact and well-suited for pattern analysis but has limited statistical power for fine-grained sub-category trend detection.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- SECTION 2: Year-by-Year Question Volume Analysis -->
  <div class="section">
    <h2>Year-by-Year Question Volume Analysis</h2>

    <p>The total number of examination questions has not remained constant across years. The analysis reveals a clear two-phase structural pattern: an early phase (2015–2016) with 27 questions per year, an expansion phase (2017–2019) with 36 questions, and a settled phase (2021–2025) with 33 questions after the pandemic gap.</p>

    <div class="chart-container">
      <canvas id="chartYearlyTotal"></canvas>
    </div>

    <h3>Detailed Year-by-Year Breakdown</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Year</th>
          <th>MCQs</th>
          <th>Short</th>
          <th>Long</th>
          <th>Total</th>
          <th>YoY Change</th>
          <th>YoY %</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>2015</td><td>15</td><td>9</td><td>3</td><td>27</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2016</td><td>15</td><td>9</td><td>3</td><td>27</td><td class="trend-flat trend-arrow">0</td><td>0.0%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2017</td><td>15</td><td>18</td><td>3</td><td>36</td><td class="trend-up trend-arrow">+9</td><td>+33.3%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2018</td><td>15</td><td>18</td><td>3</td><td>36</td><td class="trend-flat trend-arrow">0</td><td>0.0%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2019</td><td>15</td><td>18</td><td>3</td><td>36</td><td class="trend-flat trend-arrow">0</td><td>0.0%</td></tr>
        <tr><td colspan="7" style="background:#fff5f5;color:#c53030;font-style:italic;">2020 — No data available (likely COVID-19 disruption)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2021</td><td>15</td><td>15</td><td>3</td><td>33</td><td class="trend-down trend-arrow">−3</td><td>−8.3%*</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2022</td><td>15</td><td>15</td><td>3</td><td>33</td><td class="trend-flat trend-arrow">0</td><td>0.0%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2023</td><td>15</td><td>15</td><td>3</td><td>33</td><td class="trend-flat trend-arrow">0</td><td>0.0%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2024</td><td>15</td><td>15</td><td>3</td><td>33</td><td class="trend-flat trend-arrow">0</td><td>0.0%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2025</td><td>15</td><td>15</td><td>3</td><td>33</td><td class="trend-flat trend-arrow">0</td><td>0.0%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p style="font-size:12px;color:#a0aec0;">* YoY comparison for 2021 uses 2019 as the prior available year. Direct comparison with 2020 is not possible.</p>

    <div class="insight-box">
      <strong>Key Finding:</strong> The most significant structural shift occurred in 2017, when Short Questions doubled from 9 to 18 — a +100% increase — while MCQs and Long Questions remained unchanged. This suggests BIEK deliberately expanded the short-answer component to assess broader conceptual coverage. Post-pandemic (2021 onward), Short Questions settled at 15, creating a new equilibrium.
    </div>

    <h3>Three Distinct Eras</h3>
    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Era 1 (2015–2016):</strong> Compact format with 27 questions. MCQ-heavy at 55.6%, only 9 Short Questions. Represents the original exam structure.
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Era 2 (2017–2019):</strong> Expanded format with 36 questions. Short Questions doubled to 18. MCQ share dropped to 41.7%. Broader conceptual assessment.
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Era 3 (2021–2025):</strong> Settled format with 33 questions. Short Questions at 15. Near-equal MCQ/Short balance (45.5% each). Current stable structure.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- SECTION 3: Question Type Composition & Trends -->
  <div class="section">
    <h2>Question Type Composition & Trends</h2>

    <p>The BIEK Computer Science exam has exhibited remarkable stability in its MCQ and Long Question counts, while the Short Question component has been the sole source of structural variation. MCQs have been fixed at exactly 15 per year across all 10 years, and Long Questions at exactly 3 per year.</p>

    <div class="chart-container">
      <canvas id="chartStackedBar"></canvas>
    </div>

    <h3>Question Type Share Over Time</h3>
    <div class="chart-container">
      <canvas id="chartTypeShare"></canvas>
    </div>

    <h3>Statistical Summary by Question Type</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Metric</th><th>MCQs</th><th>Short</th><th>Long</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="text-align:left;font-weight:600;">Total Count</td><td>150</td><td>147</td><td>30</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="text-align:left;font-weight:600;">Mean per Year</td><td>15.0</td><td>14.7</td><td>3.0</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="text-align:left;font-weight:600;">Standard Deviation</td><td>0.0</td><td>3.3</td><td>0.0</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="text-align:left;font-weight:600;">Minimum</td><td>15</td><td>9</td><td>3</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="text-align:left;font-weight:600;">Maximum</td><td>18</td><td>18</td><td>3</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="text-align:left;font-weight:600;">Overall Share</td><td>45.9%</td><td>44.9%</td><td>9.2%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="insight-box">
      <strong>Stability Observation:</strong> With zero standard deviation for both MCQs (always 15) and Long Questions (always 3), the exam blueprint is highly rigid for these two types. The only structural variation across a decade comes from Short Questions (range: 9–18). This means educators can reliably expect 15 MCQs and 3 Long Questions in future papers, while Short Questions should be anticipated in the 15–18 range based on recent trends.
    </div>

    <h3>Composition Shift: Visual Summary</h3>
    <div class="chart-container">
      <canvas id="chartCompositionDonut"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- SECTION 4: Recurring Topics & Question Content Patterns -->
  <div class="section">
    <h2>Recurring Topics & Question Content Patterns</h2>

    <p>Keyword frequency analysis of question text reveals a dual-domain examination: C programming and Database/DBMS. These two domains account for virtually all questions, with C programming covering syntax, operators, control structures, loops, and functions, while the database domain covers DBMS concepts, MS Access, database models, and SQL.</p>

