  <!-- SECTION 6: Conclusions & Limitations -->
  <div class="section">
    <h2>Conclusions & Limitations</h2>

    <h3>Key Conclusions</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>The exam has not remained entirely stable.</strong> While MCQs (15) and Long Questions (3) have been invariant across all 10 years, Short Questions shifted from 9 (2015–2016) to 18 (2017–2019) to 15 (2021–2025), representing the only meaningful structural change in a decade.</li>
      <li><strong>Database Fundamentals is the dominant assessment domain,</strong> appearing in 94 questions across all years and all question types. Combined with MS Access, SQL/DB Languages, and Database Models, database-related content accounts for approximately 47% of all questions.</li>
      <li><strong>C Programming assessment is heavily conceptual and definitional.</strong> While loop-writing and simple program-writing appear in Long and Short Questions, the emphasis is on terminology (format specifiers, operator types, data type sizes) rather than complex programming.</li>
      <li><strong>The post-pandemic era (2021–2025) shows a stable 33-question format</strong> with near-equal MCQ/Short distribution. This is likely the blueprint for the foreseeable future.</li>
      <li><strong>Topic breadth is slowly expanding.</strong> 2024–2025 introduced bitwise operators, ternary operators, structure/union distinctions, super keys, ACID properties, views, indexes, and object-oriented database models — topics not present in earlier years.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Data Limitations</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>2020 data is missing entirely.</strong> It is unclear whether examinations were conducted that year or whether papers exist but were not included in the source dataset. Any analysis of year-over-year changes spanning 2019–2021 should acknowledge this gap.</li>
      <li><strong>Annual vs. Supplementary distinction is not available.</strong> Some years may include questions from both annual and supplementary examinations, potentially inflating question counts for those years. Without this distinction, per-paper analysis is not possible.</li>
      <li><strong>Topic classification is keyword-based and approximate.</strong> Questions were matched to topics using regular expression patterns on the question text. This approach may misclassify some questions or miss implicit topic references. The classification should be treated as indicative rather than definitive.</li>
      <li><strong>Community-sourced data quality.</strong> While the dataset appears clean and well-structured, it is compiled from scanned papers and manual transcription. Minor errors in question text may affect keyword matching accuracy.</li>
      <li><strong>Small sample size.</strong> With only 327 questions over 10 years, fine-grained sub-topic trend analysis (e.g., tracking "bitwise operators" year-by-year) lacks statistical power. Conclusions about emerging trends should be confirmed with future years' data.</li>
      <li><strong>No marks allocation data.</strong> The dataset records question counts but not marks per question. Long Questions likely carry more marks than MCQs, so question count alone may understate the assessment weight of certain topics.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Bottom Line for Educators:</strong> The BIEK Computer Science exam is structurally predictable — expect 15 MCQs, 15 Short Questions, and 3 Long Questions. Database Fundamentals and C Programming mechanics (operators, format specifiers, loops) are the highest-yield preparation areas. Watch for emerging topics (structures, pointers, advanced database concepts) that may signal curriculum evolution.
